  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening the Humanitarian Health and Water Sanitation and Hygiene Response for the Most Vulnerable Populations in Northern Abyei</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">From 12 to 16 December 2021, an inter-agency team led by OCHA undertook a Multisector Humanitarian Needs Assessment in nine locations of the central, eastern, and western corridors in northern Abyei, Sudan. The assessment further highlighted the urgent need for the implementation of multisector support including food security, protection, health, nutrition, water, sanitation and hygiene (WASH), shelter, and education to address the gaps as most families in northern Abyei have had no access to basic services for several years. This project aims to address critical humanitarian health and WASH needs of vulnerable and crisis-affected populations in northern Abyei, including internally displaced persons, pastoralists, and host communities. IOM is supporting the rehabilitation of target health facilities to provide the minimum basic package of primary health care services and capacity building for healthcare workers at facilities and within the community to ensure that people have access to skilled health staff. IOM is also providing access to improved and adequate sources of potable water for residents and livestock as well as increasing knowledge of safe garbage disposal practices and land preservation to reduce risks associated with water source contamination and disease outbreaks related to water-borne causes or poor environmental sanitation.</narrative>
  </description>
